---
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

const title = "Privacy — Machine Control";
const description = "How the Machine Control Chrome extension and project update list handle your data.";
---

<SiteLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/privacy/">
  <SiteHeader />
  <main class="article-page">
    <article class="article-header article-body shell prose-shell prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
      <h1>Your computer, your data.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Machine Control runs on your own computer. It has no Machine Control
        account, and the project does not collect what your agents see or do.
      </p>

      <h2 id="chrome-extension">Chrome extension</h2>
      <p>
        The Machine Control Chrome extension connects only to the Machine
        Control app on the same computer, through Chrome native messaging. It
        does not send data to Machine Control servers or any other remote
        service, and it contains no analytics or advertising.
      </p>
      <p>
        While you have granted browser access in the app, the extension acts on
        the requests that the app forwards from an agent you run: it can list
        tabs, open pages, read page structure, click, type, upload files you
        name, and take screenshots. With the separate DevTools grant, it can
        also run scripts and read data on any site. Results go to the app on
        your computer and from there to the agent you chose. What that agent
        does with them, including any AI service it uses, is governed by that
        agent's terms, not by Machine Control.
      </p>
      <p>
        The side panel keeps a short list of recent actions in browser memory
        so you can see what an agent is doing. It records operation names, tab
        references, outcomes, and counts, not typed text, scripts, or page
        content, and it is discarded when Chrome stops the extension. The
        extension stores only its own tab-indicator state locally. It does not
        sell or share data, and it does not use data for advertising or
        creditworthiness decisions.
      </p>

      <h2 id="newsletter">Project update list</h2>
      <p>
        If you subscribe for Machine Control updates, your email address is used
        only for occasional project news about releases, platform evidence, and
        development notes.
      </p>

      <h3>What is stored</h3>
      <p>
        The list stores your email address, when you joined, whether you joined
        from the homepage or blog, and the version of this notice. Duplicate
        signups update the existing entry.
      </p>

      <h3>Where it is stored</h3>
      <p>
        The site and signup database run on Cloudflare. Cloudflare Turnstile is
        used to reject automated submissions. The Machine Control list does not
        intentionally retain your IP address or browser fingerprint.
      </p>

      <h3>What it is not used for</h3>
      <p>
        The address is not sold, shared for unrelated marketing, or used to
        create a Machine Control account.
      </p>

      <h3>Removal and questions</h3>
      <p>
        To ask what is stored or have your address removed, or with any other
        privacy question, email
        <a href="mailto:kyle@graehlarts.com?subject=Machine%20Control%20privacy">kyle@graehlarts.com</a>.
        Future project emails will also provide a way to opt out.
      </p>

      <p>Last updated: October 13, 2026.</p>
      <p><a href="/">← Return to the homepage</a></p>
    </article>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</SiteLayout>
